{
  "problem_statement": "Within the author-created simulation, a conservatory caretaker recorded counts of two and seven leaking watering valves in personal notes later copied into a maintenance digest (C1). This supports an exploratory maintenance need, not an established recurring pattern: unknown dates, valve identities and overlap prevent treating the counts as nine distinct valves, an increase over time or independent corroboration (C2). The packet does not establish real-world need, customer validation or intervention benefit.",
  "affected_users": "The simulated conservatory caretaker who recorded the leaks (C1). Effects on other participants are not documented.",
  "observable_problem": "Two reported counts of leaking watering valves—two and seven—with no dates, valve identities, denominator or overlap information (C1, C2).",
  "urgency": {
    "frequency": "Unknown. Two recorded counts do not establish separate episodes, recurrence or a rate without dates and overlap information (C1, C2).",
    "severity": "unknown",
    "claim_refs": ["C1", "C2"]
  },
  "workarounds": [
    {
      "description": "Personal notes copied into a maintenance digest document the leaks; this is a reporting practice, not evidence of repair or successful mitigation.",
      "claim_refs": ["C1", "C2"]
    }
  ],
  "alternatives": [
    {
      "description": "A human-led inspection and dated, valve-identified maintenance record is a possible non-software investigation, not an observed or tested alternative. Existing alternatives remain unassessed.",
      "claim_refs": ["C1", "C2"]
    }
  ],
  "software_hypothesis": null,
  "cross_field": null,
  "cross_field_absent_reason": "C1 and C2 provide no supported transferable mechanism between evidence assessment and community operations. Copying notes into a digest does not demonstrate such a transfer.",
  "evidence_strength": "exploratory",
  "gaps": [
    "All observations are synthetic; internal support does not establish real-world need, customer validation or benefit (C1, C2).",
    "The notes and digest share one authorial origin, so they provide no independent corroboration (C1, C2).",
    "Unknown dates, valve identities, total valves and overlap prevent estimating recurrence, prevalence, trend or a distinct-valve total (C2).",
    "Severity and cause are unassessed; water loss, repair burden and consequences are not measured (C2).",
    "Existing alternatives and tool gaps are unassessed. The supplied catalog is empty, and no external search or testing was available.",
    "Only supplied statements are accessible. A future outcome could be dated counts of uniquely identified leaking valves, but neither a baseline nor repair outcomes are available.",
    "Defer software selection pending human inspection, identification of affected valves and assessment of existing maintenance practices; these could favor ordinary repair or process changes over software."
  ]
}

{
  "problem_statement": "Within the supplied simulation, bicycle inspections repeatedly identify loose fittings: two independent logs each record six loose fittings among thirty inspections over matching weeks using the same definition (C1). Disjoint bicycle identifiers and independent recording support corroboration within this bounded scope (C2). The observed need is to address recurring loose fittings, not a demonstrated need for software. Causes, external prevalence and intervention benefit remain untested (C3).",
  "affected_users": "Simulated participants associated with the inspected bicycles and the observers recording inspections; specific rider, owner and maintenance responsibilities are not supplied (C1, C2).",
  "observable_problem": "Each of two independent simulated logs records six loose fittings among thirty bicycle inspections during matching weeks (C1, C2).",
  "urgency": {
    "frequency": "Six loose fittings per thirty inspections in each log over matching weeks; exact dates, number of weeks and recurrence on individual bicycles are unspecified (C1, C2).",
    "severity": "unknown",
    "claim_refs": ["C1", "C2", "C3"]
  },
  "workarounds": [
    {
      "description": "Observers conduct inspections and independently log disjoint bicycle identifiers with explicit scope, denominators and matching windows. This documents detection, not an established repair workaround (C1, C2).",
      "claim_refs": ["C1", "C2"]
    }
  ],
  "alternatives": [
    {
      "description": "Prioritize human maintenance assessment of the recorded loose fittings before considering software. This is a proposed non-software direction, not an observed practice or validated intervention (C1, C3).",
      "claim_refs": ["C1", "C3"]
    }
  ],
  "software_hypothesis": null,
  "cross_field": null,
  "cross_field_absent_reason": "No supplied claim establishes a transferable mechanism between evidence assessment and community operations. Independent logging supports assessment of the need, but does not demonstrate a mechanism for improving maintenance outcomes (C1–C3).",
  "evidence_strength": "well_supported",
  "gaps": [
    "Support is confined to an author-created simulation. It establishes no real-world need, customer validation or benefit; independence is stipulated within the simulation (C1, C2).",
    "Only synthetic statements are accessible, not underlying inspection records. Matching windows are asserted, but exact dates and inspection schedules are unavailable (C1, C2).",
    "Fitting types, looseness thresholds, safety consequences and operational disruption are unspecified; severity cannot be determined (C1, C3).",
    "Counts concern loose fittings per inspection denominator, not necessarily the proportion of bicycles affected. Individual-bicycle recurrence is not established (C1, C2).",
    "Causes, repair practices, responsibility for maintenance and intervention benefit remain untested or unspecified (C3).",
    "No material contrary observation is supplied within scope; absence of contrary evidence does not justify generalization beyond it (C2, C3).",
    "No catalog entries or existing-tool comparisons are supplied, and external search is unavailable. A software gap or transferable intervention cannot be assessed.",
    "Defer software recommendation pending maintenance context and evidence of an unmet workflow need. Loose-fitting counts per inspection provide a bounded outcome, but no intervention outcome was measured (C1, C3)."
  ]
}

{
  "problem_statement": "Within the simulation, geological collection records report nine misplaced trays and nine misplaced individual specimens in a copied inventory. These are not equivalent counts: units and observation windows differ, and no mapping is available. The observable need is to reconcile record definitions and provenance before estimating the extent of misplacement, rather than treating the records as corroboration or combining their counts (C1, C2). Recurrence and operational impact are not established.",
  "affected_users": "Simulated geological collection participants who maintain or interpret the collection inventory; specific roles and numbers affected are not supplied (C1).",
  "observable_problem": "Two related records use different counting units and observation windows without a mapping, preventing a defensible combined misplacement count (C1, C2).",
  "urgency": {
    "frequency": "One discrepancy is described; observation windows and recurrence are unspecified. Neither count of nine measures frequency (C1, C2).",
    "severity": "unknown",
    "claim_refs": ["C1", "C2"]
  },
  "workarounds": [
    {
      "description": "A copied inventory exists alongside the collection count, but it counts individual specimens rather than trays; its effectiveness as a workaround is not established.",
      "claim_refs": ["C1", "C2"]
    }
  ],
  "alternatives": [
    {
      "description": "Prefer human-led reconciliation of counting units, tray-to-specimen mappings and observation windows before combining records; this is a proposed non-software response, not an observed success.",
      "claim_refs": ["C1", "C2"]
    },
    {
      "description": "Until reconciliation is possible, retain both counts separately with their units and provenance and defer any combined estimate.",
      "claim_refs": ["C2"]
    }
  ],
  "software_hypothesis": null,
  "cross_field": null,
  "cross_field_absent_reason": "No supplied claim supports a transferable mechanism between evidence assessment and community operations. A record discrepancy alone does not establish such a transfer (C1, C2).",
  "evidence_strength": "exploratory",
  "gaps": [
    "All evidence is author-created simulation material; it establishes no real-world need, customer validation or intervention benefit.",
    "The copied inventory is not independent corroboration; the packet provides only single-author support (C1, C2).",
    "Tray-to-specimen mappings and comparable observation windows are absent. The two counts cannot be combined or treated as estimates of the same quantity (C2).",
    "Recurrence, duration, collection size and consequences of misplacement are unknown; urgency and severity cannot be inferred from counts of nine.",
    "Only supplied statements are accessible, not underlying inventories. Reconciliation requires unit definitions, dated records and a mapping supplied by a responsible collection participant.",
    "No catalog alternatives, tool evaluations or transferable mechanisms are supplied. Software benefit is unassessed; defer software recommendation pending reconciliation.",
    "A future measurable outcome could be the number of record discrepancies unresolved after reconciliation, but no baseline beyond this described discrepancy or intervention result is supplied."
  ]
}

{
  "problem_statement": "Within the author-created simulation, a survey-equipment desk reports measuring instruments returned to incorrect compartments (C1). This identifies a plausible return-placement problem, but its recurrence rate, causes and operational consequences are unmeasured. Visible paired identifiers used in camera-kit storage offer an untested, non-software direction for investigation (C2). The packet does not establish real-world need, customer validation or benefit.",
  "affected_users": "Simulated participants returning measuring instruments and personnel responsible for the survey-equipment desk; specific roles and numbers are not established (C1).",
  "observable_problem": "Measuring instruments are returned to incorrect compartments at a simulated survey-equipment desk (C1); no count or denominator is supplied.",
  "urgency": {
    "frequency": "Single-author observations describe incorrect returns, but no observation period, event count or return volume establishes frequency (C1).",
    "severity": "unknown",
    "claim_refs": ["C1"]
  },
  "workarounds": [
    {
      "description": "The camera-kit procedure uses visible paired identifiers on equipment and designated compartments. This is a source-field practice, not an established survey-equipment workaround (C2).",
      "claim_refs": ["C2"]
    }
  ],
  "alternatives": [
    {
      "description": "Investigate a non-software option: pair visible identifiers on survey instruments and their compartments. Transfer remains untested; first assess whether destination ambiguity contributes to incorrect returns.",
      "claim_refs": ["C1", "C2"]
    }
  ],
  "software_hypothesis": null,
  "cross_field": {
    "from_field": "camera-kit storage",
    "to_field": "survey equipment returns",
    "transferred_mechanism": "Place matching visible identifiers on each instrument and its designated compartment so a returning participant can match the object to its destination rather than recall or infer the placement. This adapts the camera-kit procedure described in C2.",
    "why_it_transfers": "Both settings associate equipment with designated compartments. Matching identifiers could reduce destination ambiguity if that ambiguity contributes to survey return errors. That causal premise is unknown, and neither successful transfer nor reduced errors is demonstrated (C1, C2).",
    "claim_refs": ["C1", "C2"]
  },
  "cross_field_absent_reason": null,
  "evidence_strength": "exploratory",
  "gaps": [
    "All evidence is author-created and synthetic. Single-author observations lack independent corroboration and establish no real-world need or customer validation (C1, C2).",
    "Incorrect-return counts, total returns and observation duration are absent; recurrence rate and severity cannot be assessed (C1).",
    "Causes remain unknown. Paired identifiers are relevant only if participants can perceive and use them and destination ambiguity contributes to errors (C2).",
    "Survey-desk workarounds, existing tools and competing approaches are undocumented; the empty catalog is not evidence that alternatives do not exist.",
    "Only supplied synthetic statements are accessible. No instrument-level return records or data collection access are established.",
    "Transfer has not been tested. A future authorized comparison could measure incorrect-compartment returns divided by total returns; no baseline or benefit is available (C1, C2).",
    "Defer software recommendation: no software-specific mechanism or tool gap is evidenced. The next direction is human assessment of causes and the non-software identifier option, not deployment."
  ]
}

{
  "perspective": "existing_tool",
  "title": "Sort and export the title list with the installed spreadsheet",
  "mechanism": "Use the installed spreadsheet to order the synthetic book titles and export the result. C1 identifies a bounded sorting need; C2 states that the installed spreadsheet fully supports the required sorting and export. A staff member can select the title column as the sort key, keeping any associated row data together, and review the output. This addresses the stated need without a custom application.",
  "intended_users": "The simulated community reading room participants responsible for preparing the book-title list.",
  "smallest_version": "One spreadsheet containing the supplied titles and one sorted export. Preserve an unchanged input copy, choose the intended ordering convention, sort the complete data range by title, and export using the installed functionality. Review title preservation and ordering before handing over the list. The packet contains no actual titles, so this is a proposed workflow, not a completed sorted artifact.",
  "measurable_outcome": "Acceptance target: every input title occurrence is preserved, no unintended titles are added, and no adjacent pair violates the chosen ordering convention. Actual title count and results are unknown; no sorting or checks were executed.",
  "feasibility": "C2 directly supports capability fit within the synthetic scenario. The workflow needs the actual title list, the intended ordering convention, and the desired export format; these details are not supplied. No network, deployment, or new metered spending is needed. Human review can resolve conventions such as treatment of leading articles, punctuation, and case. Evidence supports this bounded recommendation, not real-world validation or measured time savings.",
  "maintenance": "Retain the original list and ordering convention. For later additions, repeat the sort and review the export. No custom code, service, or dependency maintenance is proposed.",
  "software_warranted": {
    "verdict": "no",
    "reason": "New or custom software is not warranted: C2 explicitly states that the installed spreadsheet completely supports the required sorting and export, with no evidenced capability gap. Existing functionality plus a brief human review addresses C1. A separate build would add maintenance without a supported benefit."
  },
  "recommendation": "recommend",
  "claim_refs": [
    "C1",
    "C2"
  ],
  "assumptions": [
    {
      "statement": "The installed spreadsheet is accessible to the person preparing the list, as the scenario's capability statement implies.",
      "would_change": "If access is unavailable, first consider another already available sorting tool or manual sorting rather than assuming a custom build is needed."
    },
    {
      "statement": "The intended ordering convention and export format can be specified using the installed spreadsheet's supported options.",
      "would_change": "If a concrete unsupported rule emerges, reassess that gap and existing alternatives before considering customization."
    },
    {
      "statement": "The deliverable is the bounded title list described in C1, with input occurrences preserved unless explicitly directed otherwise.",
      "would_change": "Requirements for deduplication, ongoing integration, or repeated high-volume processing would require additional evidence and a revised workflow assessment."
    }
  ],
  "novelty": null,
  "response_to_findings": null
}

{
  "perspective": "software",
  "title": "Conditional local pronunciation-symbol checker",
  "mechanism": "C1 identifies a symbol-consistency checking need on one approved synthetic file; C2 reports only that the inspected editor lacks this operation. A possible software response is a read-only local checker that compares pronunciation tokens with an explicitly approved symbol inventory and context-sensitive consistency rules, then reports suspect tokens with file locations and rule explanations. Human review would distinguish mistakes from legitimate transcription variants. This mechanism is proposed, not tested; symbol differences alone do not establish errors.",
  "intended_users": "Simulated phonetics-exercise participants reviewing the approved synthetic pronunciation file, with an exercise owner responsible for defining acceptable notation.",
  "smallest_version": "Conditional on obtaining the file format and approved notation rules, make a single-file local command that accepts only the approved input and a small rule configuration. Report out-of-inventory symbols and violations of explicitly specified consistency rules with line or token locations; do not rewrite the file or infer pronunciation correctness. Keep processing offline and exclude directory scanning, deployment and integrations. Before implementation, compare manual review and any already available local checking operation against the same task. If either suffices, stop without building custom software.",
  "measurable_outcome": "Proposed measures: reviewer-confirmed inconsistencies found, false flags, missed known inconsistencies and total setup-plus-review time versus manual review on the approved file. Baseline counts and benefits are unknown; no comparison has run.",
  "feasibility": "A small rule checker is plausible if inconsistencies can be expressed as deterministic rules, but the packet supplies neither file contents nor format, symbol inventory or adjudicated examples. Unicode combining marks, token boundaries and legitimate transcription variants may complicate matching; normalization must not silently erase meaningful distinctions. C2 establishes a gap in one editor, not a gap across existing tools. No implementation or tests were executed. Defer a build decision until authorized file access, notation rules and a bounded comparison are available; use only existing local resources with no new metered spending.",
  "maintenance": "Keep rules versioned with the exercise and have its owner review changes. Maintain a few approved examples for each rule if implementation proceeds. Avoid a service; abandon the checker if upkeep exceeds one-file review effort.",
  "software_warranted": {
    "verdict": "unclear",
    "reason": "The inspected editor's missing operation supports considering a checker, but does not justify a custom build by itself (C2). For one file, an approved-symbol checklist plus human review or an existing local search/checking facility may cost less than implementing and validating rules. Custom software becomes warranted only if explicit rules are reliably checkable and a bounded comparison shows useful coverage or lower total effort without excessive false flags. Neither comparison nor benefit evidence is supplied."
  },
  "recommendation": "consider",
  "claim_refs": [
    "C1",
    "C2"
  ],
  "assumptions": [
    {
      "statement": "An exercise owner can specify accepted symbols and distinguish legitimate variants from inconsistencies.",
      "would_change": "If notation remains ambiguous, favor human-led review and defer automated error classification."
    },
    {
      "statement": "Any evaluation remains limited to the approved synthetic file and authorized local resources.",
      "would_change": "If the file or required tools are unavailable within that scope, defer implementation rather than seek wider access or spending."
    },
    {
      "statement": "A one-file task may not recover the setup and validation cost of custom software.",
      "would_change": "If manual review or an existing tool suffices, recommend no custom build; if a bounded comparison demonstrates an advantage, reconsider a minimal checker."
    }
  ],
  "novelty": null,
  "response_to_findings": null
}

{
  "perspective": "human_led",
  "title": "Facilitated interpretation review with explicit unresolved disagreements",
  "mechanism": "C1 describes a conflict of interpretation priorities, not a demonstrated information-processing failure. Propose a human-facilitated review that separates disputes about historical evidence from differences in values, makes each member's priorities explicit, and asks the panel to choose how to handle disagreement. Members may agree on an interpretation, authorize presentation of multiple interpretations, or defer the decision; no weights or consensus are presumed. The facilitator records reasons and dissent but does not resolve value choices on members' behalf, consistent with C2. This is a proposed process, not a tested intervention.",
  "intended_users": "The simulated local history panel members, supported by a facilitator acceptable to them; members remain the final interpretive decision-makers.",
  "smallest_version": "For one disputed panel interpretation, hold one bounded facilitated session using a paper worksheet or an existing shared document. Record the candidate interpretations, the historical evidence members cite, each member's priorities and non-negotiable concerns, and areas of agreement and dissent. Ask members to explicitly approve a decision procedure before using it; do not default to majority voting or numerical weights. End with a member-authorized interpretation, an authorized plural presentation, or a documented deferral naming the unresolved choice. Circulate the record for explicit confirmation; silence is not approval. If no procedure is authorized, stop at the disagreement record.",
  "measurable_outcome": "For the single interpretation reviewed, record whether a decision procedure was explicitly authorized, whether an interpretive outcome was authorized or deferred, and the count of unresolved priority conflicts. Baseline counts and improvement are unknown; no session has been conducted.",
  "feasibility": "The proposal requires members' participation, an acceptable facilitator, their stated priorities, and any historical evidence needed for the chosen interpretation. Only C1 and C2 are supplied synthetic observations; neither establishes willingness to participate, available facilitation, or a workable decision rule. No historical materials or specific interpretations are provided, so the assignment can propose a protocol but cannot settle the dispute. Use existing meeting arrangements and recording materials if available without new spending. If those conditions are absent, defer rather than presume authority or resources. Human deliberation can clarify disagreement but may not reconcile it.",
  "maintenance": "A panel-designated recorder would preserve approved reasons and dissent, distinguish later revisions, and reopen decisions only under a panel-authorized review rule. Facilitation effort recurs when new evidence or priorities create fresh disputes.",
  "software_warranted": {
    "verdict": "no",
    "reason": "No custom software is warranted by the supplied evidence. The identified obstacle is incompatible priorities without agreed weights (C1), and members have retained value judgments (C2). A ranking engine would require choices the panel has not authorized. Paper or an existing shared document can support recording, while facilitation and panel judgment address the actual conflict. Reconsider custom support only if a separate, evidenced coordination burden emerges and members authorize a narrowly administrative function; it must not silently choose interpretive priorities."
  },
  "recommendation": "consider",
  "claim_refs": [
    "C1",
    "C2"
  ],
  "assumptions": [
    {
      "statement": "Members are willing to discuss priorities and can accept a facilitator without transferring final interpretive authority.",
      "would_change": "If participation or facilitator acceptance is absent, defer the session and retain only a proposed protocol; do not treat nonparticipation as consent."
    },
    {
      "statement": "Existing meeting time and recording materials can support one session without new metered spending.",
      "would_change": "If these resources are unavailable, defer implementation rather than assume funding or impose extra commitments."
    },
    {
      "statement": "The panel can explicitly authorize its decision procedure and determine whether plural presentation or deferral is permissible.",
      "would_change": "If an external mandate limits those options, establish that mandate before proceeding; absent a legitimate procedure, document disagreement without issuing a substantive decision."
    },
    {
      "statement": "Priority conflict is central, as C1 states, but the supplied evidence does not rule out factual disputes or unequal ability to speak.",
      "would_change": "Material evidence gaps would require historical review; coercion or exclusion would require safer participation arrangements before interpreting apparent agreement."
    }
  ],
  "novelty": null,
  "response_to_findings": null
}
